---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const path = '/alternatives/sendlane-alternative';
const title = 'Best Sendlane Alternatives for Shopify (2026)';
const desc = 'Evaluate Sendlane account continuity after the Privy acquisition, compare replacement workflows, and plan permission, sender and queue migration.';

const altSlugs = ['sequenzy', 'klaviyo', 'omnisend', 'drip', 'rejoiner', 'activecampaign', 'brevo', 'yotpo-email-sms'];
const alternatives = altSlugs.map((s) => appBySlug(s)!);

const faqs = [
  { question: 'Why do Shopify merchants leave Sendlane?', answer: 'Review the current agreement, required journeys, service use and matched total cost. Privy acquired Sendlane in 2026; ask the account team what continuity and transition options apply before choosing an independent replacement.' },
  { question: 'What is the best Sendlane alternative for SMB?', answer: 'There is no universal SMB winner. Evaluate Sequenzy for reviewed AI-assisted authoring, Omnisend for Shopify email and SMS coordination, or Klaviyo for a documented profile-data requirement. Test the required jobs and obtain matched quotes.' },
  { question: 'Will I lose migration support leaving Sendlane?', answer: 'Confirm the support and export commitments in both agreements. Assign owners for implementation, message approval, monitoring and incidents; an agent-assisted editor does not replace account support or migration acceptance.' },
  { question: 'Sendlane vs Klaviyo when leaving?', answer: 'Test a specific requirement such as variant interest with a purchase exclusion. Compare data availability, update behavior, plan eligibility, internal effort and total cost. A general data-depth ranking does not prove a reason to switch.' },
  { question: 'How does Sendlane migration work?', answer: 'Inventory forms, integrations, journeys, sender identities, and queued messages before switching. Preserve permission evidence by purpose and channel, withdrawal and suppression history, and current purchase state. Test mappings, purchase exits, and dispatch eligibility; assign one sender and queue owner per journey, drain or cancel old queued messages, and deduplicate replayed events. Advance by readiness with a rollback plan rather than a fixed overlap period. Verify available exports and number-transfer terms with the providers before depending on them.' },
  { question: 'When should I stay on Sendlane?', answer: 'Stay when the current account meets required journeys, reporting, support, reliability, and total operating cost. Test email and SMS permissions separately, current suppressions and purchase exits, and queue ownership across channels. Compare attributed revenue consistently and distinguish it from incremental margin; order volume or a merchant category alone does not establish fit.' },
  { question: 'Is Rejoiner like Sendlane?', answer: 'Rejoiner presents a retention platform with expert support. Ask which strategy, implementation and ongoing operations are included in the proposed agreement, and retain an internal approver. More service can be a valid choice when that is the missing resource.' },
  { question: 'What Sendlane features matter most on exit?', answer: 'Document the functions and service commitments actually used: onboarding, channel coordination, testing, segmentation, reporting, and integration maintenance. Verify those requirements in the destination plan and Shopify integration. Rebuild must-haves first, retain permission and suppression evidence, and include operator time and transition costs in the comparison.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/alternatives">Alternatives</a><span class="mx-2 text-slate-500">/</span><span class="text-slate-600">Sendlane alternatives</span></nav>
      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="catalog-kicker">Account continuity and platform choice</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Best Sendlane alternatives for Shopify</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-slate-800">Sendlane combines ecommerce messaging with a service-oriented approach. Before leaving, establish what your current account provides and what will change: Privy announced its acquisition of Sendlane in February 2026. Ask the account team about the actual product, support agreement, renewal terms and any transition offered to your store. An acquisition announcement does not establish a shutdown date or automatic migration of your data.</p>
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-slate-600">Seven active candidates and one historical listing follow, grouped by the job you need to replace. Consider staying when the current arrangement meets requirements; changing providers creates work even when the feature lists look similar. Separate an account-continuity discussion with Privy from an independent competitive migration.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox title="Start with the missing job" picks={[
          { label: 'Draft and review', tool: 'Sequenzy', reason: 'Trial AI-assisted authoring with an accountable approver and verified connector.' },
          { label: 'Profile rules', tool: 'Klaviyo', reason: 'Rehearse variant interest, exclusions and data freshness.' },
          { label: 'Channel handoff', tool: 'Omnisend', reason: 'Test Shopify welcome and cart coordination across eligible channels.' },
          { label: 'Service ownership', tool: 'Rejoiner', reason: 'Define the software and expert-service responsibilities in writing.' },
        ]} />
      </div>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Why Shopify merchants leave Sendlane</h2>
        <p>Cost mismatch is a reason to investigate, not a conclusion about every Sendlane plan. For a hypothetical pottery studio with four hundred monthly orders, inventory welcome, cart and post-purchase messages, actual send volume, service use and renewal terms. Obtain a matched destination quote before assuming that order count makes one supplier cheaper.</p>
        <p>Onboarding and ongoing support are different purchases. Record what the team still receives: troubleshooting, deliverability advice, implementation changes or incident response. If the operator can take over those jobs, evaluate a self-managed workflow; if the team still depends on them, price replacement services explicitly.</p>
        <p>Compare the features actually used, rather than assuming two platforms have equivalent capabilities. For example, document the event fields, exclusions, reporting windows, and support commitments behind a cart journey. Check each requirement in the destination plan before calling overlapping feature labels a reason to switch.</p>
        <p>Staying can suit a climbing-gear shop whose retention lead uses coordinated messaging and support effectively, or a vinyl label with reliable release operations. Review required service commitments and renewal notice before canceling. Neither attributed flow revenue nor an unused onboarding service alone establishes the value of the ongoing agreement.</p>

        <h2>Current supplier context</h2>
        <p>Checked November 25, 2026: <a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/blog/privy-acquires-sendlane">Privy’s acquisition announcement</a> describes a service-oriented combination; its <a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/sendlane-acquisition">Sendlane page</a> presents email, SMS and onsite tools. Confirm actual account access, capture forms, integrations, support contacts, renewal and export terms directly. Neither announcement proves a mandatory cutover or that separate accounts share live permission state.</p>
        <p>Candidate capabilities are supplier-described: <a class="link" href="https://www.sequenzy.com/">Sequenzy authoring and agent access</a>, <a class="link" href="https://www.klaviyo.com/solutions/marketing-automation">Klaviyo automation</a>, <a class="link" href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/3175406-connect-your-shopify-store-to-omnisend">Omnisend’s Shopify sync</a>, <a class="link" href="https://www.drip.com/">Drip ecommerce workflows</a>, <a class="link" href="https://www.rejoiner.com/">Rejoiner platform and services</a>, <a class="link" href="https://www.activecampaign.com/apps/shopify-integration">ActiveCampaign’s Shopify integration</a> and <a class="link" href="https://www.brevo.com/">Brevo messaging products</a>. These establish evaluation candidates, not equivalent coverage or winners. <a class="link" href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">Yotpo’s retirement notice</a> excludes its legacy Email and SMS product from a current shortlist.</p>

        <h2>Newsletters, lifecycle, and transactional moments</h2>
        <p>Inventory newsletters, lifecycle automation, and SMS coordination separately. A vinyl shop might need release broadcasts, cart reminders, and post-purchase education, each with different eligibility rules. Record what the current account implements and what the replacement must reproduce; do not infer missing segmentation or additional support fees from a workflow that has not been tested.</p>
        <p>Make the operating model explicit. A draft-authoring workflow needs someone to approve copy and activation; a profile-based segmentation workflow needs a data owner; a service engagement needs an internal approver and an incident contact. Compare those roles with the work your current account team actually performs.</p>

        <h2>Support, strategy, and self-serve transition</h2>
        <p>Leaving may transfer work to your team, another provider or an agency. Rehearse a change request: update a replenishment delay, exclude a recent purchaser, review the resulting audience and restore the previous version. Record operator effort, review rights and escalation needs before setting a maintenance schedule.</p>
        <p>Request available journey documentation and exports from the account team and record any gaps. Rebuild the flows selected by business priority and operational risk; platform changes do not guarantee portable templates, event history, consent records, or queue state.</p>

        <h2>Sendlane migration checklist</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Request journey documentation and performance reports from Sendlane</strong>.</li>
          <li><strong>Export permission evidence by purpose and channel</strong> — include source, timestamps, withdrawals, and current suppression state; confirm export availability.</li>
          <li><strong>Identify Sendlane features actually used</strong> — support, SMS, A/B, attribution.</li>
          <li><strong>Assign internal owner before cancel</strong> if moving self-serve.</li>
          <li><strong>Connect replacement to Shopify</strong> — verify sync parity.</li>
          <li><strong>Rebuild and test priority journeys</strong> — verify current purchase exits, designate one sender and queue owner, drain or cancel old queued sends, and deduplicate replays before enabling replacements.</li>
          <li><strong>Synchronize current suppressions and withdrawals</strong> — verify before dispatch and prevent duplicate ownership during cutover.</li>
          <li><strong>Migrate SMS if consolidating</strong> — verify purpose, destination and channel eligibility, number-transfer terms and queue ownership.</li>
          <li><strong>Migrate post-purchase and winback</strong>.</li>
          <li><strong>Redirect popup integrations</strong>.</li>
          <li><strong>Warm domain if infrastructure changes</strong>.</li>
          <li><strong>Retain permitted evidence under an explicit retention policy</strong> — verify export and post-cancellation access before relying on them.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Three merchant scenarios</h2>
        <p><strong>Artisan coffee — proposed authoring trial.</strong> Ask an operator to draft replenishment messages for whole-bean and ground-coffee buyers in Sequenzy, review product links and claims, and record who can approve and activate the sequence. Test that a new subscription order cancels a pending reminder. Compare the measured maintenance effort and current quote with the existing account; this is an exercise, not a reported saving or performance result.</p>
        <p><strong>Outdoor gear — proposed segmentation evaluation.</strong> Build a collection-browse scenario with variant preferences and purchase exclusions in both accounts. Compare data availability, update latency, branch behavior, and operator effort. A requirement that the current setup cannot meet may justify a move, but this example is not evidence of a Sendlane limitation or a merchant result.</p>
        <p><strong>Board games — proposed stay decision.</strong> Review a coordinated email and SMS release journey against the team’s support needs, current channel permissions, suppression updates, and queue controls. Staying can be reasonable when requirements and total costs are met. Attributed recovery revenue alone does not establish incremental value or justify a premium.</p>

        <h2>Sendlane exit economics and stack patterns</h2>
        <p>Build a twelve-month cost worksheet from current quotes and the contract, not catalog entry prices. Include platform fees, eligible audience and send assumptions, SMS destinations and usage, services, integration maintenance, operator hours, training, overlap and any remaining commitment. Value time at an explicit internal rate. Check whether a peak release month changes the quote or workload; no fixed weekly time allowance or supplier saving is established here.</p>
        <p>Choose the smallest stack that passes your required jobs. A board-game shop may prioritize release newsletters and preorder exclusions; an outdoor retailer may require collection and variant data; a coffee subscription shop may depend on renewal events from another integration. Mailchimp or Shopify’s native messaging can enter a separate shortlist if those requirements fit; prior investment does not prove a simpler destination is inappropriate.</p>

        <h2>Due diligence before canceling Sendlane</h2>
        <p>Request available journey reports and verify contractual notice and export access before cancellation. Confirm sender-number transfer requirements and provider responsibilities. Test embedded discount codes, product links, and purchase exits. Choose a cutover around operational readiness and the store calendar. Reconcile attribution definitions separately from a randomized incrementality test, whose allocation and duration should follow sample size and business risk rather than a fixed percentage.</p>
      </article>

      <section class="container space-y-12 py-16">
        <p class="catalog-kicker">Candidate briefs</p>
        <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-slate-950">Sendlane replacement briefs for Shopify stores</h2>

        <section class="card" id={alternatives[0].slug}>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-black">{alternatives[0].name}</h3>
          <div class="prose-shopify mt-6">{[
          'Sequenzy describes AI-assisted campaign and flow authoring, marketing and transactional email, and agent access. This is useful to evaluate when drafting and reviewing messages is the missing operating capacity, rather than assuming a cheaper equivalent to a service agreement.',
          'For a coffee replenishment trial, supply a message specification, inspect the draft, require approval before activation and retain a recoverable version. Verify the Shopify or subscription connector, event ownership and purchase exit separately from authoring quality.',
          'Quote the actual email volume and any additional channels or services. An editable draft does not prove event parity, deliverability or reduced support needs.',
        ].map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}</div>
          <p class="mt-4"><a class="link" href={`/apps/${alternatives[0].slug}`}>Related supplier profile</a></p>
        </section>

        <section class="card" id={alternatives[1].slug}>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-black">{alternatives[1].name}</h3>
          <div class="prose-shopify mt-6">{[
          'Evaluate Klaviyo against a documented segmentation requirement, such as variant interest with a purchase exit. Verify required data, feature eligibility, integration behavior, and plan terms in the current account.',
          'Compare equivalent billable audiences and channel usage, including operator time and integration work. Assign an owner for profile hygiene and monitoring at a cadence suited to risk.',
          'Choose only when the tested requirement and total operating cost justify the transition; platform depth alone does not establish incremental margin.',
        ].map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}</div>
          <p class="mt-4"><a class="link" href={`/apps/${alternatives[1].slug}`}>Related supplier profile</a></p>
        </section>

        <section class="card" id={alternatives[2].slug}>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-black">{alternatives[2].name}</h3>
          <div class="prose-shopify mt-6">{[
          'Evaluate Omnisend for the required cart, welcome, and channel coordination journeys. Verify available templates, channel eligibility, and plan inclusions rather than assuming a lower entry price produces a lower matched bill.',
          'Test rebuild effort with representative Shopify events and eligible test accounts. Migration speed depends on mappings, permissions, integrations, and operator readiness.',
          'Choose when required workflows and support needs pass testing at an acceptable all-in cost.',
        ].map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}</div>
          <p class="mt-4"><a class="link" href={`/apps/${alternatives[2].slug}`}>Related supplier profile</a></p>
        </section>

        <section class="card" id={alternatives[3].slug}>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-black">{alternatives[3].name}</h3>
          <div class="prose-shopify mt-6">{[
          'Evaluate Drip with a branching workflow the operator actually maintains. Verify current plan terms and required event data before making a price or capability comparison.',
          'Test the Shopify integration with the required product events and purchase exits. Reconcile reporting windows and refund handling; a dashboard total is not proof of incremental revenue.',
          'Choose if workflow usability, operational controls, and matched total cost meet the documented requirements.',
        ].map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}</div>
          <p class="mt-4"><a class="link" href={`/apps/${alternatives[3].slug}`}>Related supplier profile</a></p>
        </section>

        <section class="card" id={alternatives[4].slug}>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-black">{alternatives[4].name}</h3>
          <div class="prose-shopify mt-6">{[
          'Evaluate Rejoiner against the exact software and service scope offered. Document who owns strategy, implementation, ongoing changes, and incidents rather than treating every engagement as full outsourcing.',
          'Obtain comparable current quotes covering software, channels, implementation, services, and internal operator time; no universal retainer ranking is established here.',
          'Keep an accountable internal owner even when execution is delegated, and verify the support and response commitments in the agreement.',
        ].map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}</div>
          <p class="mt-4"><a class="link" href={`/apps/${alternatives[4].slug}`}>Related supplier profile</a></p>
        </section>

        <section class="card" id={alternatives[5].slug}>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-black">{alternatives[5].name}</h3>
          <div class="prose-shopify mt-6">{[
          'Evaluate ActiveCampaign when a documented requirement connects wholesale sales work and DTC messaging. Test customer identity, purchase events, and channel-specific permission mappings in the proposed integration.',
          'Compare the required CRM and messaging workflow with its implementation and maintenance costs.',
          'Verify specific Shopify event and action requirements in each candidate; a general depth ranking does not establish fit.',
        ].map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}</div>
          <p class="mt-4"><a class="link" href={`/apps/${alternatives[5].slug}`}>Related supplier profile</a></p>
        </section>

        <section class="card" id={alternatives[6].slug}>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-black">{alternatives[6].name}</h3>
          <div class="prose-shopify mt-6">{[
          'Brevo offers marketing and transactional messaging products. Evaluate it against the actual lifecycle requirements and current billing terms. Test event coverage, exclusions, support, and operator effort before assuming a cheaper or less capable replacement.',
          'Use a coffee-shop newsletter and a separate transactional receipt exercise: identify which product handles each, their permission rules, and which Shopify events the proposed integration supplies.',
          'Choose when verified workflow coverage and matched total costs meet the budget, with a named operational owner.',
        ].map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}</div>
          <p class="mt-4"><a class="link" href={`/apps/${alternatives[6].slug}`}>Related supplier profile</a></p>
        </section>

        <section class="card" id={alternatives[7].slug}>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-black">{alternatives[7].name}</h3>
          <div class="prose-shopify mt-6">{[
          'Historical listing: Yotpo scheduled full deprecation of its Email and SMS products for December 31, 2025. Do not treat this entry as a current migration destination.',
          'The Email and SMS notice is separate from continuing Yotpo Reviews and Loyalty products; retaining those products requires a separate messaging integration decision.',
          'Verify the current destination, event mappings, permission records, and matched all-in costs before transferring any messaging workflow.',
        ].map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}</div>
          <p class="mt-4"><a class="link" href={`/apps/${alternatives[7].slug}`}>Related supplier profile</a></p>
        </section>
      </section>

      <div class="container mb-12 max-w-4xl card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-slate-950">Matched quote worksheet</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Cost input</th><th>Record for current account and each candidate</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Messaging</td><td>Eligible contacts, monthly sends, peak campaigns, SMS destinations and overages</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Operations</td><td>Required plan features, support scope, integrations, review and incident hours</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Transition</td><td>Implementation, overlapping subscriptions, remaining contract and number transfer</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Acceptance</td><td>Required journey coverage, suppression freshness, purchase exits and reporting definitions</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="container max-w-4xl pb-8 text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-slate-950">Related guides</p>
        <p class="mt-2"><a class="link" href="/apps/sendlane">Sendlane review</a> · <a class="link" href="/compare/rejoiner-vs-sendlane">Rejoiner vs Sendlane</a> · <a class="link" href="/compare/sendlane-vs-klaviyo">Sendlane vs Klaviyo</a> · <a class="link" href="/alternatives">All alternatives</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="prose-shopify container max-w-4xl pb-8">
    <h2>Four-week Sendlane exit timeline</h2>
    <p><strong>Week 1 — Premium audit.</strong> This is an illustrative planning sequence, not a required duration. Inventory the functions and services actually used, available exports, current permissions by purpose and channel, suppressions, and source-of-truth purchase state. Assign an accountable operator and identify records that cannot be migrated safely.</p>
    <p><strong>Week 2 — Core flow rebuild.</strong> Rebuild welcome and cart in the selected destination with test orders. Document Sendlane SMS timing if consolidating channels. Begin parallel suppression tagging.</p>
    <p><strong>Week 3 — Expand and align.</strong> Test post-purchase care or setup guides using test orders and accounts. Preserve relevant VIP exclusions and verify that a later purchase exits recovery queues before dispatch. Plan any randomized holdout around detectable effects and adequate sample size; reporting continuity and causal lift are separate questions.</p>
    <p><strong>Week 4 — Cancel premium tier.</strong> Proceed only after readiness checks, regardless of the calendar label. Assign a sender and queue owner per journey, drain or cancel legacy queued messages, and deduplicate replayed events. Check fresh permission, suppression, and purchase state at dispatch. Retain a rollback route and permitted evidence under a documented retention policy.</p>

    <h2>Buyer desk: premium utilization audit</h2>
    <p>Audit used functions and service commitments across a representative cycle, including a release peak and a rare support incident. Obtain a written continuity answer from the current provider before interpreting changes in branding as changes in your agreement. A quote comparison should include the services your team would have to replace.</p>
    <p>Assign a named owner for eligibility rules, integrations, deliverability monitoring, and incident response. Estimate operator time from the actual workload and service commitments; there is no universal weekly minimum that makes a self-serve stack safe.</p>
    <p>Define success before migrating: required journeys function correctly, no duplicate sends or suppression gaps occur, reporting reconciles, and operator effort and total cost fit the budget. Observe a period suited to the buying cycle and seasonal variation. A fixed revenue tolerance or deadline cannot establish equivalent performance, and platform attribution does not measure causal margin.</p>

    <h2>Replacement jobs to evaluate</h2>
    <p><strong>Authoring:</strong> evaluate Sequenzy with an approved replenishment draft and a verified event connector. <strong>Profile rules:</strong> evaluate Klaviyo with variant interest and current purchase exclusions. <strong>Channel coordination:</strong> evaluate Omnisend with a welcome-to-cart handoff. These are different acceptance briefs, not a price or performance ordering.</p>
    <p><strong>Proposed exercise — snack box DTC.</strong> Model a subscriber who skips a box, changes dietary preferences and later cancels. Preserve the subscription system as the source of truth, distinguish promotional permission from service notifications, and prove that stale events cannot re-enroll the buyer. Reconcile event timestamps and customer identifiers before judging a replacement.</p>
    <h2>Premium feature utilization audit</h2>
    <p>List features and service commitments used across a representative business cycle, including rare but critical support incidents. Judge the replacement against those requirements and measured operator effort. A quiet month or an unused onboarding service alone does not prove that the ongoing plan is wasteful.</p>

    <h2>Internal owner replaces account manager</h2>
    <p>Give the snack-box operator a concrete handover: a runbook for paused subscriptions, the source of dietary preferences, a review log for copy changes, an incident contact and authority to pause sends. Rehearse a missed sync and a replayed cancellation event. Record how much work the rehearsal takes and assign backup coverage; no merchant outcome or universal audit duration is claimed.</p>

    <h2>Premium-to-self-serve transition</h2>
    <p>Document which onboarding deliverables and ongoing services the team depends on. Assign responsibility for data mappings, eligibility checks, monitoring, and rollback before cancellation. Estimate the workload from those tasks and test the new operating process rather than imposing a fixed weekly audit time or a thirty-day success rule.</p>
    <p>For a proposed Sequenzy trial, compare a reviewed draft with the approved message specification and retain the prior version. For a Klaviyo trial, compare the selected profile audience with the source orders and exclusions. Use the same business acceptance criteria while measuring the different work each operating model requires.</p>
    <p>Audit subscriptions, send usage, channel charges, support, integration work, migration, and operator time over the same period. Compare equivalent eligible audiences and required workflows. Include overlap and contractual commitments, and report attributed revenue separately from any controlled estimate of incremental contribution margin.</p>

    <h2>Sendlane exit desk: deliverability-first migrations</h2>
    <p>Use a deliverability and event ledger for the proposed exit. For outdoor jackets, record collection and variant identifiers and test missing fields rather than assuming the current platform cannot express the rule. For board-game preorders, distinguish order placement from fulfillment and test a cancellation before dispatch. For coffee, verify grind preference and subscription state from their owning systems. For vinyl, map any RFM labels together with their calculation date rather than treating imported tags as fresh behavior. Record authentication, send windows, complaint signals and suppression freshness before changing infrastructure. Define journey precedence explicitly, for example canceling cart recovery on purchase while preserving eligible care messages. Compare reports using identical attribution windows; a sequential migration cannot isolate causal platform lift. Assign escalation and rollback criteria from actual delivery signals rather than a ninety-day graduation clock.</p>
  </article>

    <h2>Sendlane migration: deliverability ramp calendar</h2>
    <p>Use a readiness-based deliverability plan. Inventory authentication, sender identity, reputation, engagement evidence, and provider guidance before changing infrastructure. Test transactional and promotional eligibility separately: permission for one purpose or channel does not authorize another. Start any controlled ramp with an eligible audience selected from reliable engagement and permission records, monitor delivery and complaints, and expand only as evidence supports it. Open events alone are not reliable eligibility or consent evidence. Carry current withdrawals, suppressions, and purchase exits into the destination, assign one queue owner per journey, cancel or drain old sends, and deduplicate replayed events. Pause when evidence is incomplete; importing records does not create permission.</p>

    <p>Set ramp duration from the sender change, provider guidance, audience quality, and monitored delivery signals. An aged domain does not establish a universal four-week minimum, and a fixed waiting period cannot guarantee inbox placement.</p>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Sendlane alternatives FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
